<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.1.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00059</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.1.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8f45ba6d-3465-420f-b8fa-4ce8a1d48805</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><para id="para-00001">For questions 1 – 5, use the following scenario to identify the expression, equation, or inequality described.</para>
<para id="para-00002"><quote id="quote-00001">To support a local senior citizens center, a student club sent a flier home to the \(n\) students in the school. The flier said, “Please bring in money to support the senior citizens center. Paper money and coins accepted!” Their goal is to raise \(T\) dollars.</quote></para><para id="para-00003">Choose the expression, equation, or inequality, from the list, that describes the given quantity. </para>
<section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002"><exercise id="c5eb8b61-7fbc-4523-a1ec-a4cc7ec0eba3" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00001"><para id="para-00004"> The dollar amount the club would have if they
          reached half of their goal. </para>
      <para id="para-00005">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00006">\(T+50\)</para><para id="para-00007">\(0.5T\)</para><para id="para-00008">\(0.25n\)</para><para id="para-00009">\(0.5n\)</para><para id="para-00010">\(T-0.5n\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00011">Correct! \(T\) equals the total dollar amount of the goal. Half that amount is \(.5T\).</para><para id="para-00012">Try again. Revisit activity 1.1.2.</para><para id="para-00013">The correct answer is \(0.5T\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="c30ec0ed-0821-4c1b-8232-b1d7ee553524" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00002"><problem id="prob-00002"><para id="para-00014"> The dollar
          amount the club would have if every student at the school donated 50 cents to
          the cause. </para>
      <para id="para-00015">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00016">\(T+50\)</para><para id="para-00017">\(0.5T\)</para><para id="para-00018">\(0.25n\)</para><para id="para-00019">\(0.5n\)</para><para id="para-00020">\(T-0.5n\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00002"><para id="para-00021">Correct! \(n\) equals the total number of students. Fifty cents equals .50, so the total amount raised if all students brought .50 is |(.5n\).</para><para id="para-00022">Try again. Revisit activity 1.1.2.</para><para id="para-00023">The correct answer is \(0.5n\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="8568a3fb-10b2-47c9-b1fb-06e433a3f9c2" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00003"><problem id="prob-00003"><para id="para-00024"> The dollar amount the club could donate if they made $50 more than their goal</para>
      <para id="para-00025">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00026">\(T+50\)</para><para id="para-00027">\(0.5T\)</para><para id="para-00028">\(0.25n\)</para><para id="para-00029">\(0.5n\)</para><para id="para-00030">\(T-0.5n\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00003"><para id="para-00031">Correct! $50 more than the goal of \(T\) is \(T+50\). </para><para id="para-00032">Try again. Revisit activity 1.1.2.</para><para id="para-00033">The correct answer is \(T+50\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="2cd99f9f-a7a8-4eaf-81ad-a2addfafc27b" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00004"><problem id="prob-00004"><para id="para-00034"> The dollar amount the club would still need to raise to reach its goal after every student at the school donated 50 cents</para>
      <para id="para-00035">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00036">\(T+50\)</para><para id="para-00037">\(0.5T\)</para><para id="para-00038">\(0.25n\)</para><para id="para-00039">\(0.5n\)</para><para id="para-00040">\(T-0.5n\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00004"><para id="para-00041">Correct! If each student, \(n\), raises .50 that is .50n. To determine how much is left to raise of the goal, \(T\), you could calculate \(T -.50n.</para><para id="para-00042">Try again. Revisit activity 1.1.2.</para><para id="para-00043">The correct answer is \(T-0.5n\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="0ff9050c-4f35-4b15-9898-adc0d7584f0c" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00005"><problem id="prob-00005"><para id="para-00044"> The dollar amount the club would have if half of the students at the school each gave 50 cents</para>
      <para id="para-00045">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00046">\(T+50\)</para><para id="para-00047">\(0.5T\)</para><para id="para-00048">\(0.25n\)</para><para id="para-00049">\(0.5n\)</para><para id="para-00050">\(T-0.5n\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00005"><para id="para-00051">Correct! Half of the students, \(n\), would be \(0.5n\). If they only raised .50 each, it would be \(9.5n(0.50)\) or \(.25n\).</para><para id="para-00052">Try again. Revisit activity 1.1.2.</para><para id="para-00053">The correct answer is \(0.25n\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="9a678a3e-ae5c-4af0-8b8b-767aba5e2bbb" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00006"><problem id="prob-00006"><para id="para-00054">Write an expression that represents the amount of flour required for one cake.</para><para id="para-00055"> Each of the 10 students in the baking club made 2 chocolate cakes for a fundraiser. They all used the same recipe, using \(c\) cups of flour in total.</para>
      <para id="para-00056">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00057">\(20c\)</para><para id="para-00058">\(\frac c{20}\)</para><para id="para-00059">\(c-20\)</para><para id="para-00060">\(\frac{2c}{10}\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00006"><para id="para-00061">Correct! If 10 students are making 2 cakes, they need to have 20 equal parts for an answer of \( \frac{c}{20} \).</para><para id="para-00062">Try again. Revisit activity 1.1.2.</para><para id="para-00063">The correct answer is \( \frac{c}{20} \). </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003"><exercise id="2d9c7397-563f-467a-b604-3775cea9aa44" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00007"><problem id="prob-00007"><para id="para-00064">For questions 7 - 8, determine if each is an expression or an equation that represents the English phrase</para><para id="para-00065"> 3(\(x\) − 7) = 27 </para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00007"><para id="para-00066">Correct! This is an equation because it has an \(=\).</para><para id="para-00067">Try again. Revisit activity 1.1.3.</para><para id="para-00068">The correct answer is equation. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="827ac3ee-90d0-4c8e-aa3e-5b7472aa4691" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00008"><problem id="prob-00008"><para id="para-00069"> 5(4\(y \)− 2) − 7 </para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00008"><para id="para-00070">Correct! This is an expression because it doesn’t have an \(=\). </para><para id="para-00071">Try again. Revisit activity 1.1.3.</para><para id="para-00072">The correct answer is expression. </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00004"><exercise id="143f01d5-22fa-4aa0-bb7a-c47383bb89b6" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00009"><problem id="prob-00009"><para id="para-00073">For questions 9 - 10, choose the algebraic expression that represents the English phrase</para><para id="para-00074">the
          difference of \(14x\) ² and 13 </para>
      <para id="para-00075">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00076">\(14x^2 + 13\)</para><para id="para-00077">\(14x^2 - 13\)</para><para id="para-00078">\(13 - 14x^2\)</para><para id="para-00079">\(\frac{14x^2}{13}\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00009"><para id="para-00080">Correct! The difference between two numbers is a subtraction problem. </para><para id="para-00081">Try again. Revisit activity 1.1.3.</para><para id="para-00082">The correct answer is \(14x^2-13\). You seem to be stuck who can you ask for help?</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="dccb07a2-39b9-4bbc-9703-6c1e266a5690" class="multiple-choice" id="exer-00010"><problem id="prob-00010"><para id="para-00083">the quotient of \(12x\) and 2</para>
      <para id="para-00084">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00085">\(12x - 2 \)</para><para id="para-00086">\(2-12x\)</para><para id="para-00087">\(\frac{12x}{2}\)</para><para id="para-00088">\(\frac{2}{12x}\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00010"><para id="para-00089">Correct! The quotient of two numbers is a division problem.</para><para id="para-00090">Try again. Revisit activity 1.1.3.</para><para id="para-00091">\(\frac{12x}2\)</para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
